#doc (Manual) "The CatCrypt verified compiler for cryptography" =>

🚧 _Under construction — experimental research prototype._ This compiler is part
of the CatCrypt project ([ePrint 2026/604](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/604.pdf)),
developed over many months with the help of AI. Interfaces, the covered source
fragment, and the trusted base are still evolving; a full manual audit is ongoing.
It is not a production tool.

CatCrypt compiles cryptography from a high-level source to constant-time machine
code, entirely inside the Lean 4 proof assistant, with a machine-checked proof
that the emitted bytes compute the source specification. It takes two kinds of
source — Rust extracted by [hax](https://github.com/hacspec/hax), and relational
specifications written directly in Lean — and lowers each through a stack of typed
MLIR-style dialects to an owned backend that encodes instructions to bytes for
x86-64, AArch64, and RISC-V.

The code names below are illustrative. For the full per-declaration API, see the
API reference generated by doc-gen4 and served alongside this manual.

# What the compiler guarantees

One *source-to-assembly* theorem governs the pipeline. For a source in the covered
fragment, the emitted bytes _denote the source specification_ (functional
correctness), run in _constant time_, and _preserve the source protocol's
security_ against target-level attackers — a robust hyperproperty, established by
back-translation of target contexts.

A single denotation-preserving encoding relation drives three uses at once: the
equality-saturation optimizer, the relational compiler, and the transport that
carries a protocol's security bound down to the emitted code. Optimizing,
compiling, and preserving security are three uses of one object.

Every dialect in the stack carries a Lean denotation, and every lowering is a Lean
function proved to preserve it. A well-typed term therefore denotes its
specification by construction. The end-to-end results are `rustToJasminCom_endToEnd`,
`robustCompilation_source_to_x86`, and `compile_correct_all`; `AxiomAudit.lean`
prints their axiom ledger.

# The pipeline and the two frontends

Two frontends feed one SSA `Com` intermediate representation:

- the _hax frontend_ compiles extracted Rust in a structured fragment — this is
  the path benchmarked against production crypto;
- the _relational frontend_ compiles a Lean specification directly, in the style
  of [Rupicola](https://github.com/mit-plv/rupicola): a specification is related
  to a low-level program by a denotation-preserving relation.

The `Com` framework — typed SSA dialects, each with a Lean denotation — follows
the structure of [lean-mlir](https://github.com/opencompl/lean-mlir). It shapes
the intermediate representation; code generation is CatCrypt's own owned backend
and does not depend on lean-mlir. The `PROVENANCE.md` file records the low-level
lineage (AUCurves, bedrock2, Rupicola) and its connection to Jasmin.

# The dialect stack

From `Com`, a source descends through per-family crypto dialects that mirror the
prime-IR MLIR op families — field arithmetic, modular and Montgomery arithmetic,
elliptic curves, polynomial and NTT coefficient forms, and pairing — and a
word-level Jasmin dialect. Each dialect fixes a small op set with a Lean
denotation; a lowering from one dialect to the next is a total Lean function
proved to commute with the two denotations. Chaining the lowerings composes the
per-step preservation proofs into the whole-pipeline theorem.

# The optimizer

Optimization is _verified equality saturation_. Rewrites are applied to an e-graph
of equivalent terms and a cost model selects a representative; every rewrite is an
instance of the same denotation-preserving encoding relation that drives
compilation, so an optimized term is provably equal in denotation to the input.
The optimizer and the compiler are two uses of one object, not two separate trust
surfaces.

# Correctness, constant-time, and security

The pipeline preserves three properties of a covered source:

- _Functional correctness._ The emitted bytes denote the source specification
  (`robustCompilation_source_to_x86`, `compile_correct_all`).
- _Constant time._ The emitted x86-64 has no secret-dependent control flow or
  memory addressing, as a hyperproperty of its leakage trace.
- _Security._ A protocol's security bound transports through the whole pipeline to
  the byte-level interface. Four chains carry this end to end with an axiom-clean
  ledger — Ed25519/EdDSA, Schnorr, X25519, and ECDSA. For Ed25519 the standard
  existential-forgery bound reaches the 32-byte wire interface. A wider set of
  roughly a hundred and thirty protocols is tied to the emitted code at the
  realization level.

The axiom ledger of the end-to-end theorems is Lean's standard axioms plus one
`native_decide` reflection axiom discharging an AES S-box array-size check.
Reproduce it with `lake env lean AxiomAudit.lean`.

# The backends and the trusted base

The compiler emits one owned in-Lean backend to three architectures. The
_x86-64_ backend is the benchmarked target and carries the constant-time clause.
The _AArch64_ and _RISC-V_ backends emit and run end to end with proved byte
encodings; their functional correctness is proved, while the authoritative
constant-time model is currently x86-only. For RISC-V, the emitted scalar
instruction semantics are proved equal to the official
[Sail](https://github.com/riscv/sail-riscv) specification on the emitted subset.

What is trusted is _named_ rather than per-implementation: the per-architecture
machine model the backend is checked against, and — for a security claim — the
protocol's cryptographic-hardness assumption. The byte-level instruction facts
(`decode ∘ encode`, the ADX carry-chain instructions) are proved. The Jasmin
compiler `jasminc` serves as a validation backend and links a small, tagged
residual set of benchmark rows.

# Benchmarks

The security and constant-time guarantee comes at same-tier cost; the benchmarks
are the empirical case for that, not a separate performance claim. The `bench/`
harness measures the _emitted assembly_ against the fastest deployed reference on
one host (Ryzen 7 PRO 7840U, `taskset`-pinned, RDTSC), reporting the ratio
ours/reference — smaller is better — and comparing only at matching scope.

Against _verified_ references (jasmin, libcrux, fiat-crypto, s2n-bignum, Vale) the
emitted assembly is at parity on field and bignum kernels: Curve25519 field
multiplication at 0.98× s2n-bignum, a 4-limb bignum multiply at 1.05× s2n and
0.982× the Jasmin reference, and `mpn` basecase multiplication faster than libgmp
at every size. Where the emit adopts a verified SIMD or field leaf the ratio is
~1.0× by construction; where it emits an unadopted kernel the scheduling tax is a
small factor — an in-Lean X25519 ladder at 1.61× s2n marks that modularity floor.

## Benchmarking against libcrux

The `libcrux_optimized_compare` harness pins the optimized production
[libcrux](https://github.com/cryspen/libcrux) crates (AVX2/`simd256` features on)
as the reference and pairs each against the owned emit cycle-for-cycle. Rows are
tagged for honesty:

- _meaningful_ — like-for-like, both fully pipeline-emitted in the same backend
  class;
- _gap_ — a pipeline gap (an adopted external leaf, or owned leaves whose
  whole-protocol assembly is incomplete); the ratio is a target, not a verdict;
- _n/a_ — a libcrux primitive with no owned counterpart yet.

The clean like-for-like rows: X25519 `secret_to_public` at 0.97× (the emit is
_faster_) and X25519 ECDH at 1.06×, both scalar ladders over the self-emitted ADX
field. On the symmetric side, best-variant-vs-best-variant, the emit is ahead of
libcrux — SHA-256 on SHA-NI at 0.20×, the SHA-3 / SHAKE family on a 4-way AVX2
core at 0.46×, HMAC-SHA256 at 0.30×. The honest _gap_ rows are ChaCha20-Poly1305
(the scalar Poly1305 leaf is the residual), Ed25519 sign and verify (an adopted
field leaf), and ML-KEM-768 (both halves are owned and KAT-checked, but not
yet linked in one binary: the arithmetic, packing, and branchless verify/cmov
leaves are owned and KAT-checked against PQClean, the `gen_matrix` rejection sweep
is owned through the guarded-loop backend and KAT-checked against the PQClean
`rej_uniform` oracle, and the Keccak-f1600 permutation is owned; the whole FO
encaps/decaps orchestration is also front-emitted and KAT-checked 6/6 against the
formosa reference, implicit-reject included. What remains is linking the owned
orchestration with the owned leaves in a single binary, and emitting the keygen
orchestration, which the front does not yet cover).

The harness already covers most libcrux crates — BLAKE2, ChaCha20-Poly1305,
Curve25519, Ed25519, HKDF, HMAC, ML-DSA, ML-KEM, SHA-2, SHA-3. Extending it to the
whole crate set (P-256/ECDSA and RSA against emit that already exists, and the
remaining ML-KEM and ML-DSA parameter sets, whose kernels are parametric) is
recompilation and wiring, not new proof — the sense in which adding a primitive to
this compiler is a recompilation rather than a fresh verification.

# A worked example

A covered routine travels the whole pipeline in one file. The examples under
`SecureCompilation/` and `X86Backend/` — for instance the Curve25519 field and
scalar-multiplication kernels, and `CompileFromHaxLinkExample` — take a
hax-extracted source, lower it through the dialect stack, and emit x86-64 bytes,
with the `rustToJasminCom_endToEnd` agreement discharged on the result. The
emitted bytes are the same artifact the `bench/` harness times against the
reference. Run `AxiomAudit.lean` on the endpoint to see exactly what the result
rests on.

# Scope and limitations

The hax frontend compiles a structured fragment of extracted Rust — A-normal form
with structured control flow (`IsCoveredCtl`), including free nesting of loops,
conditionals, guarded loops, and bounded `while`, and SIMD extension operations.
It is _not_ a proof that arbitrary Rust compiles; the fragment widens as the
frontend grows. The relational frontend compiles Lean specifications directly.

Some primitives are present as arithmetic and permutation leaves rather than whole
signers: HAWK is emitted at the NTRU-ring-multiply kernel level, and FN-DSA
(Falcon) is reference-linked because its discrete-Gaussian sampler is not
constant-time-emittable. The constant-time clause is anchored on the x86 model;
AArch64 and RISC-V carry functional correctness and byte encoding but not an
authoritative leakage model. The owned-emit migration has driven `jasminc` off
nearly every kernel — the bignum, symmetric, curve, and field kernels are all
owned-backend emit; a handful of advanced signature and pairing kernels (Falcon,
HAWK, MAYO signing, a BLS12-377 pairing) are the last residual, where `jasminc`
still serves as the validation backend.

# How this documentation is organized

Three layers document the compiler, read together: this _manual_ (the narrative
and worked examples), the _API reference_ (every declaration with its signature,
generated by doc-gen4), and the _proof blueprint_ (the theorem-dependency graph
linking each informal statement to its formal theorem). See `BUILDING.md` for how
to build each.
